(Banteay Meanchey): To celebrate the 5th of May Midwifery Day under the theme "Strengthen the midwifery profession to improve maternal, infant and youth health care services in the context of climate change" Cambodian Red Cross Branch Banteay Meanchey Province on 5 June 2024 This gift was given to 200 pregnant women and midwives.
The ceremony was held in the presence of Mr. Oum Rath, Governor of Banteay Meanchey Province and Chairman of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross, and was attended by Mr. Sreng Sophal, Governor of Serey Sophorn City, Sangkat Chief of Preah Ponlea and Sangkat O'Ambel in Banteay Meanchey Provincial Teacher Training College. Village 3, Sangkat Preah Ponlea, Serey Sophorn City.
Mr. Le Chansangvat, Branch Committee Member, said that today, 100 pregnant women and 100 midwives received donations from the Cambodian Red Cross Branch in Banteay Meanchey Province on May 5, Midwifery Day. All midwives of the Kingdom of Cambodia are allowed to celebrate this day on May 5 every year in accordance with the International Midwifery Day by Sub-Decree No. 240 signed on December 31, 2008.
The day aims to commemorate midwives who create babies and care for maternal and child health, establish midwives as important agents of health services, strengthen the midwifery network and midwifery capacity in close collaboration with the International Midwifery Federation, and increase staff relations. The Ministry of Health at all levels and development partners with the same direction in maternal and child health care and highlight the importance of the midwifery profession.
Midwives play an important role in the preparation and implementation of decisions, proclamations, regulations and laws related to the work of all midwives practicing in Cambodia, both Cambodian and foreign law, as well as public and private services, NGOs Through the Royal Decree on the Establishment of the Cambodian Midwifery Committee to ensure that midwives have adequate skills and provide quality services to women, mothers, children and infants.
Mr. Oum Rath continued that all midwives will receive additional skills to be able to provide services to women, mothers, infants, children, youth, improve health and well-being to help Cambodia achieve sustainable development goals. Scheduled for 2030.
