(Phnom Penh): Professor Chheang Ra, Minister of Health, expressed his deep concern over the sale and distribution of e-cigarettes online and the use of e-cigarettes among young people.
This statement was made by the Minister on the occasion of presiding over the celebration of World No Tobacco Day 2024 under the theme "Protecting children from tobacco products" on June 5, 2024 at the Cambodiana Hotel. The event was also attended by Dr. Marianna Trias, WHO Representative in Cambodia.
The Minister of Health said that the Ministry of Health has recently been concerned about the e-cigarette distribution and e-cigarette distribution among young people. Electronic cigarettes, HTPs and SISHAs are new products developed by the tobacco industry and advertised as non-toxic products like regular cigarettes or smoking cessation tools.
"According to the World Health Organization and many studies, these products have serious health effects, such as adverse effects on adolescent brain development and cancer," he said. Consumers are easily addicted to various drugs in the product. "In addition, the introduction of e-cigarettes could also pave the way for young people to use tobacco and other drugs."
On behalf of Prof. Chheang Ra, Minister of Health and Chairman of the Tobacco Products Inspection Committee, I would also like to express my deep gratitude to the relevant ministries, the Tobacco Product Inspection Committee, the Capital-Provincial Administration, the World Health Organization and all relevant partners for Efforts to actively promote tobacco control in Cambodia have been proudly successful in reducing the risk factors for Cambodians from non-communicable diseases.
This World No Tobacco Day 2024 under the theme "Protecting children from tobacco products", the Minister also called on all stakeholders to protect children from tobacco products and e-cigarettes by promoting law enforcement. Educate more widely about the effects of tobacco products and e-cigarettes and promote the implementation of a smoke-free environment.
The Royal Government, under the right leadership, with a long-term vision and realism of the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Hun Manet, has focused on the development of human capital and the welfare of the people. The Ministry of Health considers the prevention of non-communicable diseases as a top priority, in which the control of tobacco products is an important part of the prevention of non-communicable diseases.
