(Phnom Penh): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KICA) in Cambodia has issued a press release following the launch of the "Road to Digitalization: Cultivating Information Technology Talent" project.
In a press release obtained by Fresh News on the morning of May 5, 2024, Kayaka stated that human capital is one of the most important foundations for sustainable economic growth and diversification. The Royal Government of Cambodia has been focusing on the importance of human capital development, and it has become the first corner of the first phase of the Pentagon strategy recently announced by the Royal Government.
Among the many qualifications, digital skills related to information technology are very important for individuals looking for opportunities in the job market, especially in light of the rapid growth in digital technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI). However, there are still challenges to the skills gap between the quality of training and the labor market needs.
In this sense, the way to narrow the gap is a question that everyone asks. The Cambodian Customs Office has initiated various projects to address this challenge.
The project also provides digital skills training to students from secondary to tertiary (higher education) so that they have IT skills.
The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KICA) is a bilateral aid agency of the Government of the Republic of Korea. Since opening its office in Phnom Penh in 2003, KOICA Cambodia has implemented a number of development cooperation programs in the fields of education, health and rural development.
During a recent leadership meeting between His Excellency Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Yoon Suk Yeol, President of the Republic of Korea, through a joint statement on the establishment of a strategic partnership between the Republic of Korea and the Kingdom of Cambodia. "Training the next generation with industry and digital skills to drive change and innovation in the industry" is key.
A Memorandum of Understanding on the Skills and Talent Development Program was signed on that occasion. The two countries will further cooperate on human capital development.
Prior to this agreement, KOICA implemented a project with the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training on "Capacity Building and Creating an Environment in Information Technology for Vocational Training in Cambodia", which was launched in 2023. . This is also highlighted in the joint statement, which has the following meaning.
Recognition of science, technology and innovation (STI) as a core sector for socio-economic progress. The two sides agreed to increase cooperation in this area.
More importantly, the curriculum will be reviewed through consultations with the private sector, which is a potential employer for students. This activity will promote the digital skills of young Cambodians and bridge the gap between labor force capacity and labor market needs.
Public vocational training institutes in the provinces of Cambodia have applied to become target schools for project implementation. The Project Steering Committee, chaired by the Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, together with the Co-Chair of KOICA Cambodia, will soon review and announce the results of the 20 target schools.
On behalf of the Government of the Republic of Korea, KOICA is committed to taking responsibility for strengthening the partnership between the two countries and as a bridge between the two peoples, and will continue to do so forever.
