ASEAN Committee on Science, Technology and Innovation Holds a Meeting to Accelerate the Implementation of Regional Science and Technology Priorities in Siem Reap
(Siem Reap): The ASEAN Committee on Science, Technology and Innovation held its 85th meeting on 4 June 2024 in Siem Reap. The meeting aims to promote regional cooperation and accelerate ASEAN's scientific and technological progress.
After hearing Brunei's report as the outgoing chair on the achievements of 2023, Cambodia as the new rotating chair for 2024 presented the vision for further cooperation and strengthening the region's innovation system and discussed strategic goals for the future. .
The meeting heard the report of the results of the previous meeting, which provided the context and set the strategic direction of the committee. The outcome of the meeting was used for a series of discussions regarding past decisions, status of implementation of the ASEAN Foundation for Science, Technology and Innovation, scholarships and initiatives in science, technology and innovation.
The meeting reviewed the Advisory Council Report, the ASEAN Science, Technology and Innovation Action Plan (APASTI) for 2016-2025 and discussed the adoption and key points to guide the Committee's strategic initiatives and establish a basis for Develop future plans, including the APASTI Action Plan 2026-2035.
In preparation for the 20th ASEAN Ministerial Meeting on Science, Technology and Innovation (AMMSTI-20), the COSTI Meeting decided to adopt the ASEAN Agenda and Joint Statement, which are consistent and agree on scientific, technological and strategic priorities. Innovation for the public and a wide range of international stakeholders.
The COSTI-85 meeting demonstrates the ASEAN Member States' commitment to accelerating the implementation of ASEAN's science, technology and innovation priorities by establishing a framework for cooperation and promoting sustainable and inclusive regional progress. Kampong Cham Prosecutors Meet to Promote Law Enforcement and Take Strong Measures Against Gangs
