At the same time, the Minister also inspected the progress of the restoration of Prek Peam Satha of the project to remove water and divert water from Phnom Penh, from Boeung Ta Mok to the exit of Prek Peam Satha, about 19 kilometers long, located in Khan Prek Pnov, Phnom Penh. And Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province. The project was launched in mid-December 2023. So far, the work progress has reached about 65% and is scheduled to be completed by the end of August 2024.
Deputy Prime Minister Sun Chanthol, First Vice President of the Council for the Development of Cambodia, has made great efforts to protect the reputation of the Funan Techo Canal project on the international stage. Defending the positivity of the canal project, Mr. Sun Chanthol gave a series of interviews with the international media.
On the occasion of attending the international forum on "Future of Asia" organized by Nikkei in Tokyo, Japan, these days, Mr. Sun Chanthol also used this forum to promote and protect the reputation of Funan Techo canal. Sun Chanthol's speeches were also published by the Nikkei.
Not only that, Mr. Sun Chanthol also wrote an article on the Fun Techo canal project to publish on the Nikkei newspaper. The construction of the Funan Techo canal is Cambodia's prerogative because it is on Cambodian soil, but the canal was built for international trade, so protecting the reputation and values of the canal is a must.
In an article published on the Nikkei on May 25, 2024, the Deputy Prime Minister highlighted the title, "Cambodia's Maritime Road Project Will Bring Prosperity." "This waterway will be a new vehicle for trade and cooperation, not for any conflict or threat," he said.
Stretching 180 km from the heart of Phnom Penh to the quiet coast of Kep province in the Gulf of Thailand, the Funan Techo Integrated Water Resources Management Project was approved in May 2023. The project will modernize Cambodia's ancient waterways and bring a new era of economic prosperity and sustainable development.
The restoration of the waterways used by the ancient Funanese empire more than 2,000 years ago, widening to a diameter of 100 meters and a depth of 5.4 meters, allows up to 3,000 tons of ships to pass through. The rehabilitation will include the construction of three sluice gates and 11 bridges.
Environmental advocates, social planners and geopolitical analysts have thoroughly examined the proposed water management system project. The well-being of the Mekong River ecosystem, the livelihoods of local communities and the balance of regional dynamics are issues that the Cambodian government considers with the utmost responsibility.
This ambitious and prudent waterway project is based on a commitment to economic efficiency, environmental sustainability and regional stability.
By establishing direct shipping lanes for trade, the waterway project will reduce Cambodia's reliance on land transport and foreign ports, which in turn reduces shipping costs and boosts Cambodia's role in Global and regional markets.
The strategic importance of this waterway will attract more foreign investment into Cambodia, especially in the areas of transportation, logistics and manufacturing, which can take advantage of more favorable transportation.
The project will provide a number of social benefits, including increased access to education and health care, improved food and water security, improved transport and logistics services for agricultural products, and reduced flood risk. And drought and many other important services for the people in the area.
At every stage of the project, community feedback is taken seriously and the well-being of the people is prioritized, with joint planning and equity reparations at the forefront of our efforts. This new infrastructure is for all Cambodians, a joint effort that will ensure common prosperity.
A thorough environmental impact assessment conducted with 48 international experts will ensure that the new waterway respects the natural heritage of the Mekong River and complies with the 1995 Mekong Agreement signed by Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam.
In the spirit of full transparency, Cambodia informed the Mekong River Commission in August 2023 of the details of our project and the results of our study, confirming our commitment to cooperation and accountability.
In addition, the initiative will improve water management in five provinces in Cambodia by reducing the occurrence of droughts and reducing flash floods in the country and reducing the risk of flooding. Regional floods could also affect Vietnam.
Cambodia is a peace-loving country and is highly committed to maintaining regional stability and common prosperity. I can confirm that this Funan Techo Integrated Water Resources Management Project will operate under the sacred principles of the Constitution of Cambodia, established on the basis of territorial integrity and sovereignty, and strictly prohibiting the presence of foreign troops or military bases on its territory. .
In short, this waterway will be a new vehicle for trade and cooperation, not for any conflict or threat. We are committed to implementing this project that will definitely change the face of Cambodia with the strong support of the Cambodian people at home and abroad.
This is a testament to Cambodia's unwavering commitment to socio-economic progress, environmental protection and sustainable development for our nation, our people and our region. Banteay Meanchey Provincial Armed Forces Send Two Gangsters to Court After Detention and Found a Knife
