Tomorrow, the AIDS Health Care Foundation will hold a Menstrual Health Day to jointly announce the end of women's menstrual cramps.
The event will be held at Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen Chambok Meas Secondary School, Sangkat Bak Kheng, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh. More than 300 teachers.
Menstrual Health Day raises awareness of the importance of menstrual health education and resources such as underwear that will help him attend school, reduce stigma and discrimination and allow them to grow and develop.
It is estimated that 1.8 billion people worldwide menstruate, but 500 million will experience poverty during the menstrual cycle, including a lack of access to menstrual health products, safety and hygiene and sanitation. Accept the community. These barriers, along with stigma, can lead to poor health outcomes, absences from school or work, and negative effects on mental health. This Health Day, End Poverty and End Stigma.
Dr. Houy Sikheng, Country Program Manager, said that improving menstrual health for girls' schoolgirls could lead to long-term outcomes on women's education, health and well-being as a whole.
The AIDS Health Care Foundation (AHF) is the world's largest organization working on HIV / AIDS. It currently provides medical care and / or services to more than 1.7 million clients in 45 countries worldwide, including the United States, Africa, Latin America / the Caribbean, Asia / Pacific and Europe.
