A South Korean military official has revealed that both South Korean and US intelligence authorities are closely monitoring the activities in Dongchang-ri, North Korea's main space center in the northwestern part of the country. However, specific details about the arrangement have not been released.
North Korea launched its first spy satellite last year, claiming that leader Kim Jong Un examined images taken from high ground, including the White House and the Pentagon. However, state media did not release any satellite imagery.
The initial launch sparked sanctions from the United States and its allies, prompting Seoul to suspend parts of the 2018 military agreement with Pyongyang. In retaliation, North Korea canceled the treaty and built a previously demolished guard post near the border. The isolated state has also promised to launch three more satellites this year.
North Korea has defended its satellite program as a legitimate exercise of its self-defense rights, criticizing Washington's reaction as a double standard regarding South Korean satellite launches. Chairman of Svay Leu District Local Working Group Leading the Closing Ceremony of Capital, Provincial Council, Provincial Council, Municipal Council, District Council and Khan Council of the 4th Mandate
