Cambodia has ambitions to expand the tiger population to more than 100 if the first phase of the big tiger rehabilitation project is successful
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia has ambitions to expand the distribution of more than 100 big tigers if the first phase of the Big Tiger Rehabilitation Project is successful.
The Big Tiger Rehabilitation Project is a joint project between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of India based on the recent signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the two countries as a historic step forward for conservation. Big tiger.
For this project to restore the big tiger after disappearing from the territory of Cambodia in 2007 by hunting for trade in bones and skins. Its presence has been found in areas such as the northeast, southwest and north of Cambodia.
Mr. Chea Samang said that the Ministry of Environment, together with the Wildlife Alliance, has seriously considered the hardships of the people living in the area around the Cardamom Mountains, where the big tigers are released. Therefore, before entering the natural forest, the Ministry of Environment together with experts has set up a system to monitor the movement of tigers to ensure the safety of tigers and people.
Mr. Asamang said: "This is the first step, we will continue this work plan, because in the Cardamom Mountains may be able to expand the population of more than 100 tigers. When the success of the Tiger Restoration Program is a success of biodiversity conservation and stabilizing the Cardamom Ecosystem, we will turn this Cardamom Mountains into a potential recreation area for you. "Tourism."
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment also expressed hope that through national and international journalists will continue to spread the importance of the presence of big tigers in Cambodia to the people and the public.
"During the tenure of the Indian Ambassador to Cambodia, the big tiger will be present in the Cardamom Mountains of Cambodia, and the big tiger will last forever and be able to expand to other areas." This is an additional expression of Mr. Chea Sam Ang's confidence.
In addition, the Indian ambassador said that after the successful delivery of four large tigers in the first phase, the Indian government will consider providing up to 11 more.
Dr. Sou Vanna stated, "Our organization directly protects the 1.7 million hectares of Cardamom Rainforest in order to protect and conserve the latest untouched rainforests in Asia. Southeast. Forests, wildlife and humans can live together in harmony. "We have to work hard to achieve this."
The Ministry of Environment and the Wildlife Alliance (WA) have set aside 90 hectares of forest land in the Tatai Wildlife Sanctuary of the Cardamom Mountains, with iron fences, iron cages, ponds and wildlife feed for large tigers. The previous Indian government no longer entered the core area of the protected area.
