Cambodia, France Strengthen Cooperation on Water Management Project in Siem Reap-Angkor
Cambodia's economy and employment have grown rapidly in recent decades due to macroeconomic stability, trade openness and access to Western markets that have helped the country attract foreign direct investment. However, the quality of work is still low, with 98% of jobs in the country being low-skilled and a large part of it being informal.
For five years, the "Skills for Better Employment" project has been designed to help Cambodia diversify its economy into areas that require more advanced skills. This project will be implemented by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training and the Skills Development Fund.
"As Cambodia strives to step up its value chain and diversify its production, improving current and future workforce skills will be a chore," said Mariam Salim, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. "A key to attracting more and better foreign direct investment and better responding to the needs of employers."
"Technical training that responds to additional needs by training skills such as digital skills and behavioral skills can help Cambodia achieve its goals," she said.
Samdech Thibodi Hun Manet Chairs the Semester Meeting of the Supreme Advisory Council
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of Friday, May 24, 2024, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, His Excellency Hun Manet, presided over the semi-annual meeting of the Supreme Advisory Council.
The meeting was held at the Peace Palace, the Prime Minister's Office. The meeting started with the opening remarks of the meeting and the importance of the contribution of the delegates of the Supreme Party, the Advisory Council and the Advisory Council by Samdech Mani Hun Manet.
The meeting also provided an opportunity for each party delegate to make constructive contributions to the social situation over the past six months and the vision and expectations of each party delegate.
It should be noted that the Supreme Advisory Council was initiated by Samdech Techo Hun Sen as Prime Minister in 2018 at the beginning of the 6th legislature, which is a plane for consultation and advice of political parties to the Royal Government outside the parliamentary framework. In order to enhance the efficiency of work for the benefit of serving the people and the potential development of the society.
The Supreme Advisory Council may also propose or advise the Prime Minister on the actual needs of the preparation, amendment of the law or the amendment of other legal documents to the Prime Minister for review and submission to the relevant Minister, Ministry or Secretariat.
The Supreme Advisory Council may give an opinion on the draft law and other draft legal documents prepared by the Ministry or the Secretariat to the Prime Minister before the Council of Ministers.
Members of the Supreme Advisory Council may also submit the results of their work to the Advisory and Advisory Agenda in any meeting of the Supreme Advisory Council and may also submit directly to the Prime Minister for review and approval. . The Supreme Advisory Council may also suggest improvements to the Prime Minister's inactivity or irregularities.
