(Phnom Penh): The Government of Cambodia, India and the Wildlife Alliance have announced a strategy to rehabilitate big tigers in Cambodia. The first phase of handing over four large tigers is expected to take place during November or December 2024.
However, the big tiger was last spotted on camera in 2007. By 2016, the big tigers had been officially declared extinct by the Cambodian government. The extinction of this species is caused by illegal hunting for sale on the international black market as a drug or medicine.
Captive tigers kept in India will be sent to Cambodia by the Indian Air Force and then transported to the Big Tiger Rehabilitation Station in Koh Kong province, a new sanctuary in the Cardamom Mountains. The area has been successfully protected for two decades under the auspices of the Wildlife Alliance and the Ministry of Environment.
The executive director of the Wildlife Alliance has confirmed that the four big tigers from India, which are planned to be brought to Cambodia in the first phase, if Cambodia succeeds in caring for and conserving the big tigers, the government will Increase the offer to 11 more. The four big tigers will be released in the core of the Cardamom Mountains, which is protected and considered the safest way to rehabilitate big tigers in Cambodia.
A senior official from the Ministry of Environment added that the project is a large-scale project with cooperation between Cambodia and India. The Ministry of Environment is working closely with Cambodian partners India and the Wildlife Alliance on this project. At the same time, he is excited that the big tiger will arrive in Cambodia soon. Bringing big tigers to Cambodia's wilderness is a long journey that takes years.
"I know we all look forward to the arrival of the big tiger. Anyway, let me tell you that even if the big tiger arrives in Cambodia, we still have a lot of work to do. "We all need to work together to protect the big tigers so that they can live in harmony in their natural habitat in the Cardamom Forest." This is what Mr. Chea Sam Ang said.
The Ambassador of the Republic of India to Cambodia said that the delivery of the big tiger could take place during November or December. However, the repatriation of tigers to Cambodia may take longer, depending on the weather and other factors. And the delivery of this big tiger is the first step that her country gave to Cambodia, and if this project is successful, India will increase the supply to 11 more.
The Ministry of Environment and the Wildlife Alliance (WA) have set aside 90 hectares of forest land in the Tatai Wildlife Sanctuary in the Cardamom Mountains, with iron fences, iron cages, ponds and wildlife feed for large tigers. The previous Indian government no longer entered the core area of the protected area. Stung Sen Water Supply Authority, Kampong Thom Province, announces the participation in bidding for the supply of equipment to expand the network within the service area.
