(Pursat): On the morning of May 23, 2024, at Wat Hangsa Bei, also known as Wat Prek Sdey, leaders of civil servants, youth, the Armed Forces, monks and people in Pursat Province celebrated the National Day on May 20 to commemorate To the souls of the people who lost their lives during the genocidal regime of Pol Pot.
The event was attended by Lol Mak Ra, Chairman of the Provincial Council, Mr. Pov Piseth, Acting Governor of Pursat Province, as well as members of the Provincial Council, Deputy Governors, leaders of various departments around the province, local authorities, government officials, teachers, and the people of Ta Chi. Grandmothers, students and young people in large numbers.
Please be informed that the National Day of Remembrance on May 20 is a memorial service for the more than 3 million victims who lost their lives during the Pol Pot genocidal regime. Every Cambodian will never forget the Democratic Kampuchea regime, also known as the Pol Pot genocidal regime (April 17, 1975 - January 6, 1979), which was a brutal and dark regime.
The National Day of Remembrance on May 20 was designated by the National Assembly of the People's Republic of Cambodia in 1983 as the "Day of Anger" to commemorate the Khmer Rouge regime's massacre of the Cambodian people. May 20, 1973 was the birthday of the cooperatives of the Khmer Rouge Democratic Kampuchea regime, and the war continued until it reached Phnom Penh on April 17, 1975.
"National Day of Remembrance" has just been designated by the Royal Government of Cambodia as a national holiday on February 14, 2018. This day is designated as an official national holiday to commemorate the victims and victims of the crimes committed during the Democratic Kampuchea regime from April 17, 1975 to January 6, 1979.
