KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim said on Tuesday that Malaysia would start cutting fuel subsidies to strengthen its fiscal position, starting with diesel, a move that would save about money. 4 billion ringgit ($ 852 million) annually. According to Reuters.
Anwar has repeatedly vowed to turn away from all-inclusive subsidies and move towards a system with a clear goal that largely helps low-income groups.
Malaysia subsidizes fuel, cooking oil and rice among other commodities, but rising commodity prices have seen rising spending in recent years hurt the government's budget.
According to Reuters, the savings from the subsidy deduction could be used for the needy, including cash aid for diesel car owners such as farmers and small business owners. .
"I reiterate that targeted subsidies should not be a burden on the majority of the people," Anwar said in a televised address. He added that the diesel subsidy reform will only apply to consumers in the Malaysian peninsula.
He did not give a date for when the subsidy reduction would take effect, saying only more details would be announced later.
According to the government's budget for 2024, Malaysia is projected to spend RM52.8 billion on subsidies and social assistance this year, down from RM64.2 billion. In 2023. According to Reuters.
