(Israel): The Israeli Foreign Ministry announced on Wednesday that it had issued an urgent order for its ambassadors to Ireland and Norway to return to Israel in response to the two countries' decision. Recognizing the independence of Palestine. This is according to the release by Reuters on Wednesday, May 22, 2024.
Israeli Foreign Minister Ehud Olmert has said that recognizing Palestine as a country is tantamount to denying Israel's right to self-defense, as well as efforts to free the 128 hostages held by Hamas in the Gaza Strip. "Israel will not stand idly by," Katz said. "We are committed to achieving our goals by restoring security for the people, defeating Hamas and bringing the hostages back."
On Wednesday, three European countries, including Norway, Spain and Ireland, recognized Palestine as a country and expects other countries to join.
Industry Minister: Integration, Integrity and Innovation Are Key to Business Development
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Hem Vandy, Minister of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, encouraged women entrepreneurs to enter the system of integrity and innovation to promote the development of micro and small enterprises in Cambodia.
Mr. Hem Vandy encouraged this on the occasion when he met with representatives of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association to discuss cooperation and support to empower women entrepreneurs in Cambodia on May 21, 2024. During the meeting, the President of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association, Ms. Chan Pheary, introduced the new leadership of the Association, as well as the vision, activities and challenges that women entrepreneurs are facing.
Mr. Hem Vandy said that the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association is an outstanding association and encouraged the new leadership of the association to take innovations to support more Cambodian women entrepreneurs to be more successful.
He also informed the delegation about ASEAN's goal of harmonizing standards by 2025, which will require products to meet those standards before they can be exported to any country in the region. He asked the association to promote access to the system among its members, as well as to cooperate with ministries and partners such as Cambodian entrepreneurs to promote the development of small and medium enterprises in Cambodia.
"In order to be competitive, it is important that we organize our business into a system that captures standards and tests product quality properly," he said.
He added that the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation is also reforming its national laboratory to increase testing capacity, focusing on the specific needs of the private sector.
Ms. Chan Pheary explained the challenges faced by Cambodian women entrepreneurs, most of whom are micro and small businesses, and have little understanding of export standards and procedures.
She said some members of the association face challenges such as declining sales, low profits and high operating costs, so the association, which currently has about 1,000 members, is working to facilitate product matching through business networking and marketing. Participate in various product exhibitions.
Mr. Hem Vandy recommended that the Association focus on institutional governance and membership representation, requesting the Association to expand cooperation with development partners such as Australia. He stressed the importance of accessing the business system to increase access to finance and skills development opportunities.
In addition, he asked the association to develop guidelines on "do's and don'ts" for those who want to become entrepreneurs, especially new businesses, focusing on business integrity. He recommended the implementation of self-monitoring through the association by organizing a business integrity competition to encourage members to conduct business ethically.
The Minister also advised the association members to consider consolidating to benefit from the dimensional economy through the mobilization of production forces and the development of production clusters. At the same time, he called for innovation in the production of traditional products such as silk can be designed not only for garments, but also to other products such as interior decoration, hotels or large buildings to increase the market for those products.
About 1,000 monks and Buddhists march in Pisak Bochea ceremony in Sambo district, Kratie province
(Kratie): Preah Serey Samphear, Dr. Eng Phalla, Honorary Council of the Kingdom of Cambodia, Preah Anukon, Sambo District and Preah Chao Athikar Wat Preah Vihear Thom Sorsor 100, and Mr. Kong Meta, Governor of Sambo District, led colleagues, principals, teachers, students and people. About 1,000 Buddhists attend the Pisak Bochea procession in Sambo district, Kratie province.
The event was co-organized by Sambo District Sub-School and Sambo District Administration at Wat Preah Vihear Thom 100 Pillars and marched on public roads in the district town.
Dr. Eng Phalla Mean said that the celebration of Pisak Bochea is to commemorate three special events related to the Buddha: those births, enlightenment and Nirvana.
He added that the Buddha was originally named Sitha (pronounced Sit-thot), the son of Sirim Mahamaya and King Sototanak. He was called by many other names, such as: Samma Samput, Samanakotam, Jinsrey, Sakyamuni, Preah Sastra, etc. He was born on Friday, March 623, when there was At the age of 29, Preah Setha went to build a tower.
Vesak is an important Buddhist festival that was recognized by the United Nations as the International Vesak Day in 1999, allowing the festival to be held directly at the UN headquarters. Buddhists around the world organize the festival according to the traditions of each country.
In particular, Vesak is considered a national festival of Cambodia, where all citizens take a break from work to celebrate this festival. Vesak is celebrated to commemorate three important events:
The Buddha was born in Lumpini Park (now Lumpini in Nepal) under a tree on Friday, the 15th day of the month of Pisak in the year 600 BC.
At the age of 29, observing the old age, sickness, death, and wandering in the never-ending cycle of love, the Dharma (Sothot) found a way out of those memories. After six years of searching, he found a way to end all suffering and attained enlightenment as a Buddha at the age of 35 on Wednesday, the 15th day of the full moon of the month of Pisak in the year of Roka.
He then spent 45 years explaining how human beings, angels, and all beings understand how to alleviate suffering in life. All traces are born of jealousy, envy, jealousy, resentment, and ignorance of nature. If one tries to give up those misfortunes, one will attain true peace.
He has spent his whole life trying to save the world without discriminating against anyone. His advice has been an effective medicine to heal the suffering of the world from that time until today.
He passed away at the age of 80 on Tuesday, the 15th day of the full moon of the month of Pisak, in the year of the Snake, leaving his teachings, the Dharma, as a beacon for all beings.
Vesak is the day on which he was born, enlightened, and Nirvana. The Vaisakhi festival is held to commemorate His Majesty the King, who sacrificed everything in his life to save all beings. Japan is closely monitoring the value of the yen.
"On the other hand, if prices continue to be high, it will be difficult to reach this goal despite rising wages."
Although a weaker yen has benefited exporters, it has become a headache for Japanese policymakers due to the impact on consumption, which has pushed up the cost of importing raw materials. .
The yen weakened to more than 160 yen to the dollar late last month, raising suspicions that the Tokyo government had intervened, according to Reuters. The Japanese currency has rebounded since then, trading at about 156.45 yen per dollar.
