Vesak is an important Buddhist festival that was recognized by the United Nations as the International Vesak Day in 1999, allowing the festival to be held directly at the United Nations. Buddhists around the world organize the festival according to the traditions of each country.
In particular, Vesak is considered a national festival of Cambodia, where all citizens take a break from work to celebrate this festival. Vesak is celebrated to commemorate three important events:
The Buddha was born in Lumpini Park (present-day Lumpini in Nepal) under a shrine on Friday, the 15th day of the month of Pisak in the year 600 BC.
At the age of 29, observing the old age, sickness, death, and wandering in the never-ending cycle of love, the Dharma (Sothot) found a way out of those memories. After six years of searching, he found a way to alleviate all suffering and attained enlightenment as a Buddha at the age of 35 on Wednesday, the 15th day of the month of Vesak in the year of Roka.
He then spent 45 years explaining to humans, angels, and all beings how to alleviate suffering in life. All traces are born of jealousy, envy, jealousy, resentment, and ignorance of nature. If each of us tries to let go of those misfortunes, we will find true peace.
He carried out a lifelong mission to save the world without discriminating against anyone. His advice has been an effective medicine to heal the suffering of the world from that time until today.
He passed away at the age of 80 on Tuesday, the 15th day of the month of Vesak, in the year of the Snake, leaving behind his teachings, the Dharma as a light to enlighten the world.
Vesak is the day on which he was born, enlightened, and Nirvana. This Vaisakhi festival is held to commemorate His Majesty the King, who sacrificed everything in his life to save all beings. Moldova signs security, defense partnership agreement with EU
