(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia on May 20, 2024 presented the report on financial stability in 2023. In which, Dr. Chea Serey, Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia presented some important points of the evaluation of the report on financial stability in 2023.
On the morning of May 20, 2023, the National Bank of Cambodia held a workshop on "Financial Stability Report 2023" with the participation of Dr. Chea Serey, Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia.
Dr. Chea Serey said that this is the sixth annual report released by the National Bank of Cambodia to contribute to raising awareness of the economic and financial situation and provide a perspective on financial stability.
Dr. Chea Serey stated that today, I would like to highlight some important points of evaluation in the 2023 Financial Stability Report as follows:
* Regional and global economic conditions are assured to be resilient despite rising interest rates and high global inflation. In this situation, the Cambodian economy continues to grow at a rate of 5% with growth at different speeds across sectors. Meanwhile, the inflation rate dropped to 2.1% in line with regional inflation trends and falling oil prices in international markets.
* Raising the US benchmark interest rate has pushed the US dollar higher against many international currencies, but pressure on the riel has been kept in check, stabilizing the exchange rate through the supply of foreign currency. Appropriate levels, intervention in the foreign exchange market and the use of various monetary policy instruments with extreme caution.
At the same time, international reserves are kept at a reasonable level, which can guarantee the import of goods and services for the next seven months, higher than the minimum three months that developing countries should have. External debt increased slightly, but the debt-to-GDP ratio decreased due to higher economic growth than external debt growth.
* The Cambodian banking system remains strong despite external pressures, with the banking system's assets and deposits continuing to grow, but weak growth in some sectors and rising interest rates have led to a slowdown in credit demand. Increased vigilance by banks and financial institutions in lending has slowed credit growth to one-digit rates for the first time in more than two decades.
However, rising interest rates have also pushed deposits up to 13% by 2023. In the current banking system, commercial bank assets account for more than 91% of total assets after a large deposit-taking microfinance institution was merged with a banking institution last year as part of its commitment. Efforts of the National Bank of Cambodia to strengthen the resilience of the banking system.
Dr. Chea Serey said that the slowdown in credit growth coupled with the completion of credit restructuring has caused the non-performing loan ratio to increase rapidly but remains low. However, this growth requires banking and financial institutions to increase regular monitoring and assessment of risks to financial stability.
At the same time, rising provisions and interest rate spending have reduced profits in the banking system. Overall, based on the test results, the Cambodian banking system maintained resilience while the publicity and liquidity ratios continued to remain high, albeit with some differences at the institutional level.
"However, we must remain vigilant, especially against the weakness of the oversupply real estate sector, and the housing price index in the second half of 2023 has declined while declining investment flows," she said. Direct from abroad to this sector.
In this situation, in order to manage the risks in the real estate sector as well as to avoid and reduce the potential impact on the financial stability, all relevant authorities will continue to closely monitor and monitor the risks in the real estate sector and the interconnected risks. Of the real estate sector.
Dr. Chea Serey stated that in the last decade, the non-banking financial sector has continued to grow rapidly, with the capitalization of the equity and bond markets, as well as the assets in the insurance sector have continued to grow steadily. Riel bonds were also issued and contributed to the use of the national currency and debt sustainability.
On the other hand, the use of electronic payment systems has increased rapidly, especially after the Kovid-19 crisis, in which payment transactions through the Bakong and KHZO codes have increased significantly, which has contributed to the facilitation. Trade, tourism, investment and remittances, as well as improving the financial environment.
