EU launches probe into China's steel trade
(Brussels): The European Commission has launched an investigation into sheet metal or tin-plated steel products from China to assess whether imports to the EU are being sold at exorbitant prices. According to Reuters.
The probe, along with another that began Thursday on imports of wood for flooring, is the latest in a series of trade and EU subsidies on exports. China and Chinese companies operating in Europe, especially the investigation against subsidies on electric vehicles.
EU-US probe and tariff levies have sparked a backlash from Beijing, which said on Thursday that China and the United States have accused China of "Europe" being selling goods at a lower price. Out of their overcapacity in the European and US markets are the "clear trade protection" of the two countries.
The investigation into the tin will last up to 14 months, with the possibility of a temporary tax deduction of seven to eight months.
According to Reuters, the European Iron Association Eurofer said the opening of the investigation was an important step towards restoring fair competition. The association added that EU industries lost a quarter of sales between 2021 and 2023, while Chinese imports more than doubled.
The Chinese Chamber of Commerce in the European Union said it was concerned the results of the investigation could affect Chinese industry and exports in the future. The Chinese Chamber of Commerce said it was particularly concerned about the EU's practice of setting a "normal price" based on what the EU said was a base price that did not affect market prices. This normal price is compared with the export price to determine the extent of the distortion.
In most cases, distortion is simply the sale of an export product at a lower price than the exporter's domestic market. According to Reuters.
