Journalists in Cambodia: Study visit to technical schools will make society more aware of 1.5 million training programs
(Phnom Penh): About 20 local journalists from 10 institutions in Cambodia said that by participating in their outreach, the Royal Government of Cambodia's target of 1.5 million vocational training programs will be expanded. .
The statement was made during a visit to the National Institute of Polytechnology of Cambodia (NPIC) organized by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training to provide journalists with an opportunity to learn about vocational and technical training programs for young people. From 1.5 million poor and vulnerable families.
An Kuntheara, a reporter for Fresh News, emphasized that the current visit of journalists is very important for journalists as well as the general public, because through their writings or articles, journalists will make society accessible. The information is clearer and more accurate.
"I see that the main point of this 1.5 million program, in addition to helping to train and educate young people in the first phase, is true, but I see that it is in line with the vision of the Royal Government," he said. Of course, the hearts of journalists always want to be close to the source of information, so this visit is really important for journalists.
As a journalist, Mr. An Kuntheara really enjoys participating in disseminating this important program to the general public.
Meanwhile, Ms. Choun Raksmey, a reporter for the CCT Times, also noted the importance of the journalists' visit to the NPIC, claiming that the dissemination will add to the information that the school has to the people well and widely.
Ms. Choun Raksmey stated: "I hope that the release of articles or videos of journalists will be able to bring this message to the public and target citizens. "I hope the Ministry of Labor will continue to lead journalists to other institutes because their coverage has an impact on the public."
In particular, Mr. Sam Ngim, a journalist from BTV, believes that the participation of journalists will promote the program of 1.5 million people of the Royal Government of Cambodia so that the target youth to study more.
"This program includes female students, male students and older students," he said. I think all these skills are very important for them from poor families. "I hope the Ministry of Labor will continue to expand all these activities."
It should be noted that this is the second visit of a journalist to the National Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia (NPIC) and the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training organized and led by HE Kata On, Secretary of State and Spokesperson to promote the dissemination of the training. More technical and professional skills. Ministry of Labor: The Cambodia Daily's broadcast is untrue and unprofessional
"This broadcast is a fabrication of false information that misleads, slanders, defames the honor and dignity of the head of government and HE Heng Sour, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training. "Unprofessional acts, unethical journalism and irresponsibility."
For the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, this is not the first time that the Cambodia Daily has published such untrue and exaggerated information as a stepping stone to extremist political activities, painting and attacking the head of the Cambodian government and Heng Sour only for his worship. The Cambodia Daily must publicly apologize and correct its coverage immediately.
